                {
                    "file": "extensions/amd-event-log/README.md",
                    "line": 111,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Ideally this would list the actual fields, or are those not known ahead of time?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "extensions/amd-event-log/README.md",
                    "line": 111,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jayanth Othayoth",
                        "email": "ojayanth@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ojayanth"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks Matt, good point.\nThe intent is to have a set of well-known AEL fields (like VERSION, TYPE, AFID, etc.) that are defined ahead of time and used by consumers.\nAt the same time, we allow adding extra AEL fields for flexibility.\nI\u2019ve updated the document to clearly list the core fields and mention that extensions are allowed. Let me know if you think it should be stricter."
                },
                {
                    "file": "extensions/meson.build",
                    "line": 5,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "not that it should be a problem, but I use the standalone meson builds for this repo a lot for dev and testing, so that still needs to work with any new code"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meson.options",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Many years ago when I put in the one below, I had to go into openbmc-build-scripts/setup.py and then enable it there to get test coverage.\n\nNow I'm not sure if that was the best route, or if it was to have it default to enabled,but then disable it if part of a subproject and also then just have a packageconfig for it to enable it it bitbake."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meson.options",
